  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\nAnomalously strong mid level high will extend across the lower Great\nLakes today and then become reoriented in a northwest-southeast axis\nthat will bring it across the Ohio Valley on Wednesday. This will \nallow the warming trend to continue. By Wednesday, heat indices will \nbe around 100, which may require a heat advisory for at least part of\nthe area.\n\nThe ridge will weaken a bit on Thursday and get nudged further\nsouthwest. 850 mb temperatures are forecast to be a degree or so\nlower than Wednesday, but surface temperatures may not be much\ndifferent than persistence. Further suppression of the ridge will\noccur into the latter part of the week as troughing over eastern\nCanada into the northeast US causes northwest flow to become\nestablished across the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. There will be\nincreased cloud cover and the potential for convection, so there will\nbe a slow downward trend in maximum temperatures and heat indices.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nThere are increasing signals that a weak short wave may move out of\nthe upper Mississippi Valley and approach the region later Friday. In\naddition, there are some indications that a weak boundary may sag\nsouth into the area by that time. While overall forcing is weak, the\nenvironment will be unstable and quite moist, as precipitable water \nstarts to approach 2 inches again, so it will not take much to get \nconvection to develop. NBM PoPs have made a pretty significant swing \ntowards a wetter solution. \n\nIncreasing northwest flow into the weekend will bring additional\ndisturbances across the region which will be sufficient to result in\nmore showers and thunderstorms. Do not expect the weekend to be a \nwash out, but it will be unsettled.",
